Metab Disord. 2020;25:1–13. doi: 10.1007/s11154-020-09615-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Ferraù F, Ceccato F, Cannavò S, Scaroni C. What we have to know about corticosteroids use during Sars-Cov-2 infection. J Endocrinol Investig. 2020;28:1–9. doi: 10.1007/s40618-020-01384-5. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Ortola A, Crespo I, Ruiz-Gracia T, Gomez-Hoyos E, Cuesta M, Penso R, et al. The use of an oral salt load followed by furosemide in the treatment of euvolemic SIADH-induced hyponatraemia. In BioScientifica; 2015 [cited 2020 Aug 30]. Available from: https://www.endocrine-abstracts.org/ea/0037/ea0037ep672 27. Ruiz-Sánchez JG, Meneses D, Álvarez-Escolá C, Cuesta M, Calle-Pascual AL, Runkle I. The Effect of the Dose of Isotonic Saline on the Correction of Serum Sodium in the Treatment of Hypovolemic Hyponatremia. Journal of Clinical Medicine. 2020;9(11):3567. https://www.researchgate.net/publication/329923614_Hyponatraemia_hipovolemica_dosis_umbral_de_suero_salino_fisiologico_para_evitar_sobrecorreccion [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 28. Shoaf SE, Bricmont P, Mallikaarjun S. Effects of CYP3A4 inhibition and induction on the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of tolvaptan, a non-peptide AVP antagonist in healthy subjects. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2012;73(4):579–587. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2125.2011.04114.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Schmitt C, Kuhn B, Zhang X, Kivitz AJ, Grange S. Disease-drug-drug interaction involving tocilizumab and simvastatin in patients with rheumatoid arthritis. Clin Pharmacol Ther. 2011;89(5):735–740. doi: 10.1038/clpt.2011.35. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Tomasoni D, Italia L, Adamo M, Inciardi RM, Lombardi CM, Solomon SD, et al. COVID 19 and heart failure: from infection to inflammation and angiotensin II stimulation. Searching for evidence from a new disease. Eur J Heart Fail. 2020;15. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 31. Muhsin SA, Mount DB. Diagnosis and treatment of hypernatraemia. Best Pract Res Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2016;30(2):189–203. doi: 10.1016/j.beem.2016.02.014. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Rondon-Berrios H, Argyropoulos C, Ing TS, Raj DS, Malhotra D, Agaba EI, Rohrscheib M, Khitan ZJ, Murata GH, Shapiro JI, Tzamaloukas AH. Hypertonicity: clinical entities, manifestations and treatment. World J Nephrol. 2017;6(1):1–13. doi: 10.5527/wjn.v6.i1.1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Widmer P, Maibach R, Künzi UP, Capaul R, Mueller U, Galeazzi R, et al. Diuretic-related hypokalaemia: the role of diuretics, potassium supplements, glucocorticoids and beta 2-adrenoceptor agonists. Results from the comprehensive hospital drug monitoring programme, berne (CHDM) Eur J Clin Pharmacol. 1995;49(1–2):31–36. doi: 10.1007/BF00192355. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement not applicable.',861,'2025-10-09 07:22:07','2025-10-09 07:22:07');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(835,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11667278','Trends, outcomes and knowledge gaps in mobile apps for reproductive endocrinology and infertility: a scoping review protocol.','Dos Santos ER, de Gouvêa AR, Castro NAASR, Souza Menezes JD, Querino da Silva M, Cristóvão HLG, Martins CC, Oliveira JG, Fucuta PDS, Werneck AL, Filho GMA, Lima ARA, Caldas HC, Brienze VMS, André JC, Oliani AH.','2024','synthesising levac narratively symul kruglova shaia domar hamidzadeh muehlmann tomczyk levac sf1 surname initials narratively verbi contextualisation deadlines finalisation zelia preto famerp prepublication famerp aline russomano gouvea alinerussomanogouvea natalia arnaldo natalia famerp famerp matheus querino matheusquirino landin cristovao cristovao famerp cintia canato cintiacanato1 gisleine gisleine famerp fucuta fukuta lins werneck alexandrelinswerneck gerardo gerardo famerp regina lima09 heloisa cristina caldas heloisa famerp vania sabadoto brienze vania brienze hospitaldebase julio cesar julio famerp helio oliani oliani famerp takei ooab013 jamiaopen ooab013 curchoe repro emjreprohealth borght wyns clinbiochem domar dcns klrooney s12905 hohmann marriott girling obst gynaeco kruglova dawadi infotility e28136 gelgoot lahuec domar rbmo symul wac hillard shaia farag chyjek hamidzadeh salehin naseri boori abadi s43043 mdj muehlmann tomczyk s11930 bayley contracept landman levac mdj xeb ajn naj paez jebm babineau c14 tufanaru aromataris ajn naj popay sowden esrc borenstein hedges deardorff jmla moumane idri nafil qos diffserv moumane idri codit moumane idri abran springerplus s40064','Abstract Abstract Introduction Infertility is a complex condition that affects millions worldwide, with significant physical, emotional and social implications. Mobile apps have emerged as potential tools to assist in the management of infertility by offering features such as menstrual cycle tracking, ovulation prediction, fertility education, lifestyle modification guidance and emotional support, thereby promoting reproductive health. Despite promising advancements such as the development of apps with sophisticated algorithms for ovulation prediction and comprehensive platforms offering integrated fertility education and emotional support, there remain gaps in the literature regarding the comprehensive evaluation of mobile apps for reproductive endocrinology and infertility. This scoping review distinguishes itself by systematically mapping and synthesising evidence on mobile apps specifically for reproductive endocrinology and infertility, unlike previous reviews that may not have focused exclusively on this intersection. This review aims to analyse recent trends, identify outcomes used to assess usability and highlight knowledge gaps. Methods and analysis This scoping review will adhere to the methodological frameworks outlined by Arksey and O''Malley and Levac et al , alongside the Joanna Briggs Institute’s recommendations. The research question, ‘What is known about mobile apps in reproductive health and infertility?’ was formulated using the Population, Concept, Context (PCC) strategy. We will include studies published from January 2019 to April 2024 in English, Portuguese and Spanish that focus on the use of mobile apps in reproductive health and infertility. Searches will be conducted across electronic databases (EMBASE, ERIC, PubMed, Science Direct, Scopus and Web of Science) and grey literature sources. Two independent reviewers will carry out the study selection using Covidence software. Data extraction will follow a standardised format and will be synthesised narratively using tables and graphs. Expert consultations will be conducted to validate the findings. Ethics and dissemination Ethical approval is not required for this scoping review as it will only use published data. The findings will be disseminated through publication in a peer-reviewed journal, conference presentations and stakeholder meetings. The review protocol will be registered with the Open Science Framework to ensure transparency and reproducibility. Keywords: General endocrinology, Social Media, Internet, REPRODUCTIVE MEDICINE STRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS OF THIS STUDY. The scoping review employs a rigorous methodological framework, adhering to the Joanna Briggs Institute guidelines for scoping reviews. The review process involves a comprehensive search strategy across multiple electronic databases and grey literature sources. Two independent reviewers will conduct the study selection process to ensure reliability and reduce bias. The study will use Covidence software for efficient management of references and data extraction. Potential challenges include the heterogeneity of study designs, which may complicate data synthesis and interpretation. Introduction Infertility is a complex and multifaceted condition that affects millions of individuals worldwide, with significant implications for the physical, emotional and social well-being of those affected. 1 2 Defined as the inability to conceive after 1 year of regular, unprotected sexual intercourse, infertility can be caused by a variety of factors, including hormonal issues, chronic diseases, environmental factors and lifestyle. 3 In addition to the impact on reproductive capacity, infertility can also have profound psychological consequences, such as stress, anxiety, depression and social isolation. 4 In recent years, there has been a growing interest in the use of digital technologies, particularly mobile apps, as tools to assist in the management of infertility and the promotion of reproductive health. 5 6 These apps offer a wide range of features, from menstrual cycle and ovulation tracking to fertility education, lifestyle changes and emotional support. 7 The popularity of these apps can be attributed to their convenience, accessibility and ability to provide personalised information and support on-demand. 8 Despite their potential, the regulation of mobile health apps remains a developing area, with varying standards and guidelines across regions. This lack of consistent regulation poses challenges in ensuring the quality and reliability of health apps, making it a critical consideration for this study. Previous studies have explored the potential of mobile apps in the context of infertility, highlighting their role in education, fertility tracking and emotional support for users. 9 10 For example, Symul et al 11 evaluated the evolution of menstrual health status through mobile apps for fertility awareness, demonstrating that these apps can provide valuable insights into women’s reproductive health. Similarly, Kruglova et al 8 developed a mobile app to support infertility patients in navigating the world of infertility, addressing their informational and emotional needs. Despite these promising advancements, there remain significant gaps in the literature regarding mobile apps for infertility. Shaia et al 12 emphasised the need for a more comprehensive evaluation of the available mobile apps for reproductive endocrinology and infertility professionals to ensure their quality, reliability and clinical relevance. Furthermore, Meyers and Domar 10 highlighted the importance of examining the role of mobile apps in managing the psychological effects of infertility, an area that has received less attention compared with the medical aspects of the condition. In a recent narrative review, Hamidzadeh et al 13 suggest that e-health interventions, including mobile apps, have the potential to address the diverse needs of individuals with infertility, from providing information and education to improving psychological well-being and increasing social support. However, the authors also emphasise the need for more high-quality research to strengthen the evidence base and guide the implementation of these interventions in clinical practice. In this context, a scoping review is necessary to map and synthesise the available evidence on mobile apps in the field of reproductive endocrinology and infertility. This type of review is particularly suitable when seeking to identify the types of evidence available in a given field and analyse knowledge gaps. 14 Unlike a systematic review, which focuses on a specific research question and assesses the effectiveness of interventions, a scoping review adopts a broader approach, mapping the key concepts, sources of evidence and types of evidence available in a research area. 15 Previous reviews, such as that by Ford et al 5 conducted a scoping review of the information provided by fertility mobile apps, highlighting the variability in the quality and comprehensiveness of the information presented. Muehlmann and Tomczyk, 16 in turn, conducted a systematic review and quality assessment of mobile apps for sexual and reproductive health education, identifying the need for improvements in quality and the involvement of experts in the development of these apps. However, there is still a need for a more comprehensive review that specifically examines mobile apps for patients and users of reproductive endocrinology and infertility, mapping recent trends and identifying the outcomes used to assess the usability of these apps. This scoping review will fill this gap, providing an overview of the available evidence and identifying areas for future research and app development. Objectives The objectives of this scoping review are to: Identify and map the existing studies on mobile apps for patients and users of reproductive endocrinology and infertility. Analyse recent trends in this research area, including the types of apps developed, target populations and outcomes assessed. Identify the outcomes used to evaluate the usability, effectiveness and impact of these apps on reproductive health and user well-being. Identify and analyse knowledge gaps in this field, highlighting areas that require further research and investigation. By achieving these objectives, this scoping review will contribute to the advancement of knowledge on the use of mobile apps in the management of infertility, providing valuable insights for researchers, healthcare professionals and app developers. The results of this review may guide the development of more effective, user-centred and evidence-based mobile apps, with the potential to improve reproductive health outcomes and quality of life for individuals affected by infertility. Moreover, this scoping review may identify priority areas for future research, such as the need for high-quality studies to assess the long-term effectiveness of mobile apps in managing infertility as well as the importance of involving end-users in the app development and evaluation process. 17 This information will be crucial to ensure that mobile apps for infertility meet the needs and preferences of users, thereby promoting greater adherence and engagement. Another important aspect that this scoping review may address is the issue of regulation and certification of mobile apps for infertility. With the growing number of apps available on the market, it is essential to establish guidelines and quality standards to ensure that users have access to reliable and evidence-based information. 18 This review may provide insights into best practices and recommendations for the development and evaluation of mobile apps for infertility, contributing to the establishment of a safer and more trustworthy ecosystem. In conclusion, this scoping review on mobile apps for patients and users of reproductive endocrinology and infertility represents an important step towards understanding the current state of evidence in this rapidly evolving field. By mapping recent trends, identifying knowledge gaps and providing insights into the usability and effectiveness of these apps, this review will contribute to the advancement of research and clinical practice in the management of infertility. The results of this review may guide the development of more effective and user-centred mobile apps, with the potential to improve reproductive health outcomes and quality of life for individuals affected by infertility. Methods This scoping review will be conducted in accordance with the methodological frameworks proposed by Arksey and O''Malley 14 and Levac et al , 19 incorporating the recommendations of the Joanna Briggs Institute 20 for scoping reviews. The review process will follow these stages: Identifying the research question The guiding research question is: ‘What is known about mobile apps in reproductive health and infertility?’ This question was established using the PCC strategy, 21 where: Population: mobile apps. Concept: effectiveness. Context: reproductive health and infertility. Identifying relevant studies Inclusion criteria: studies published between January 2019 and April 2024, in English, Portuguese and Spanish, addressing the use of mobile apps in reproductive health and infertility. This scoping review will consider literature from all regions worldwide, without restriction to specific countries or continents, to ensure a comprehensive understanding of global trends and practices. Primary studies, systematic reviews, meta-analyses and clinical guidelines will be considered. Exclusion criteria: studies outside the scope, duplicate publications, letters to the editor, editorials and opinion articles. Table 1 presents the inclusion and exclusion criteria in detail. Table 1. Inclusion and exclusion criteria. Inclusion criteria Exclusion criteria Studies published between January 2019 and April 2024 Studies outside the scope of the review Studies in English, Portuguese and Spanish Duplicate publications Studies addressing the use of mobile apps in reproductive health and infertility Letters to the editor, editorials and opinion articles Primary studies, systematic reviews, meta-analyses and clinical guidelines Studies that do not meet the inclusion criteria Open in a new tab Source: Authors. Justification for timeframe The timeframe of January 2019 to April 2024 was selected to capture the most recent advancements and trends in mobile app technology for reproductive health, reflecting the rapid evolution and increased adoption of digital health solutions during this period. Study selection Databases: searches will be conducted in EMBASE, ERIC, PubMed, Science Direct, Scopus and Web of Science, along with grey literature sources. 22 Search strategy: the search strategy was developed through a comprehensive review of the existing literature and consultation with experts in reproductive health. Keywords were selected based on their relevance to the research question and were combined using Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to ensure a broad and inclusive search. Example search terms include “Mobile apps”, “Reproductive Health” and “Infertility”. Table 2 presents an example of the search strategy adapted for the PubMed database. Table 2. Search strategy in PubMed. Database Search strategy PubMed (‘Mobile apps’ OR ‘Application, Mobile’ OR ‘Applications, Mobile’ OR ‘Mobile Application’ OR ‘Mobile Apps’ OR ‘App, Mobile’ OR ‘Apps, Mobile’ OR ‘Mobile App’) AND (‘Reproductive Health’) AND (‘Infertility’ OR related terms) Open in a new tab Source: Authors. An online supplemental file containing the complete search strategies for all databases, registers and websites used in this review is available (SF1). This includes detailed descriptions of the search terms, filters and limits applied. Access to this file will allow researchers to understand and replicate our search methodology, enhancing the transparency and reproducibility of our findings. Process: the study selection process will be performed by two independent reviewers to minimise bias, using Covidence software 23 for efficient management. To further reduce bias, any discrepancies between reviewers will be resolved through discussion and consensus, with the involvement of a third reviewer if necessary. Additionally, the search strategy will be comprehensive, covering multiple databases and grey literature to ensure a wide range of studies are considered. The selection process will follow the recommendations of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses and will be presented using a flowchart. 24 Data extraction Standardised form: data will be extracted using a standardised form, developed specifically for this review, which includes information on authorship, year of publication, country, objective, study design, population, intervention details, outcomes assessed, main results and conclusions. 25 To ensure and measure the quality of the included studies, we will employ a quality assessment tool appropriate for scoping reviews, focusing on the methodological rigour and relevance of each study. Although scoping reviews typically do not include a formal quality assessment, we will ensure transparency and provide a narrative description of the methodological quality of the studies. Table 3 presents a suggested data extraction instrument. Table 3. Data extraction instrument. Item Description Authorship Surname and initials of the authors Year of publication Year in which the study was published Country Country where the study was conducted Objective Main objective of the study Study design Type of study (clinical trial, cohort, case-control, cross-sectional, etc.) Population Characteristics of the studied population (age, sex, health condition, etc.) Intervention Details of the mobile application used (name, functionalities, duration of the intervention, etc.) Comparator Details of the comparator group, if applicable (usual care, another application, etc.) Outcomes Primary and secondary outcomes assessed in the study Results Main results found, including effect measures and confidence intervals, when available Conclusions Authors'' conclusions based on the study results Open in a new tab Data synthesis Data synthesis will be performed both qualitatively and quantitatively, following a structured approach 26 , 28 : Qualitative analysis Step 1: data extraction—extract qualitative data using a standardised form, capturing key themes and concepts from each study. Step 2: thematic analysis—conduct thematic analysis to identify recurring themes and patterns across studies. This will involve coding the data and grouping codes into themes. Step 3: narrative synthesis—synthesise the findings narratively, providing a descriptive summary of the themes and how they relate to the research questions. Software: MAXQDA (Verbi GmbH, V.24, 2023) will be used to facilitate qualitative data management and analysis. Quantitative analysis Step 1: data extraction—extract quantitative data related to study outcomes, sample sizes and effect measures. Step 2: descriptive statistics—perform descriptive statistical analysis to summarise the data, including frequencies, means and SD. Software: statistical analyses will be conducted using SPSS for descriptive statistics. Consultation with experts Consultations with experts in reproductive health and infertility will validate findings and provide additional insights, 19 contributing to the interpretation and contextualisation of results. Protocol registration The review protocol will be registered in the Open Science Framework (OSF) to ensure transparency and reproducibility. 29 Discussion on knowledge gaps While our review identifies several knowledge gaps in the evaluation of mobile apps for reproductive endocrinology and infertility, addressing these gaps could lead to significant improvements in reproductive health outcomes. For the usability of mobile apps, it is essential to consider design guidelines and the challenges faced in developing effective user interfaces. For instance, studies highlight the importance of intuitive design to maximise the adoption of health applications. 30 Moreover, usability evaluation, including rigorous testing, is crucial to enhancing user experience and ensuring that applications meet users’ needs. 31 These approaches are fundamental to developing mobile apps that are not only functional but also accessible and easy to use. For instance, integrating standardised usability evaluations such as ISO 25010 and ISO 25062 32 could enhance app reliability and user satisfaction, potentially leading to increased adherence and better health outcomes. Moreover, addressing gaps in the psychological support features of these apps could improve emotional well-being, reduce stress and enhance the overall quality of life for individuals experiencing infertility. By focusing on these areas, future research and app development can create more effective tools that not only support medical aspects but also address emotional and psychological needs, ultimately leading to comprehensive reproductive health solutions. Impact on the creation and assessment of mobile apps The conclusions of this scoping review will have significant implications for the development and evaluation of mobile apps in the field of infertility and reproductive endocrinology. Here are the key areas to incorporate into the discussion: Enhancing app functionality and design User-centred design: emphasise the importance of involving end-users in the design process to ensure that apps meet the specific needs and preferences of individuals dealing with infertility. Comprehensive features: recommend incorporating features that address both medical and psychological aspects, such as stress management tools and community support forums. Standardised evaluation criteria Usability and effectiveness: advocate for the development of standardised criteria to evaluate app usability and effectiveness, ensuring consistency and reliability in app assessments. Clinical relevance: encourage the integration of evidence-based practices within apps to enhance their clinical relevance and utility. Stakeholder recommendations Collaboration with healthcare professionals: suggest partnerships between app developers and healthcare providers to ensure apps are clinically sound and aligned with current medical guidelines. Regulatory compliance: highlight the need for adherence to regulatory standards to ensure apps provide safe and reliable information. Future research directions Longitudinal studies: recommend conducting long-term studies to assess the sustained impact of mobile apps on reproductive health outcomes. Diverse populations: encourage research that includes diverse demographic groups to ensure app applicability across different populations. Patient and public involvement None. Ethics and dissemination This scoping review does not involve primary data collection and will only include data from previously published studies. Therefore, ethical approval is not required. All data used in this review are publicly available and have been obtained through ethical research practices as reported in the original studies. Dissemination The findings of this scoping review will be disseminated through various channels to reach a broad audience. This includes publication in a peer-reviewed journal, presentations at relevant conferences, and meetings with stakeholders in the field of reproductive health and infertility. Additionally, the review protocol will be registered and made publicly accessible via the OSF to promote transparency and reproducibility in the research process. Timeline Planning and deadlines This scoping review will be conducted over a period of 6 months, starting in September 2024 and with an expected completion in February 2025. The detailed timeline is presented below: Estimated dates for each phase of the study October 2024: finalisation of the protocol and registration in the OSF. November 2024: database searches and initial study selection (screening of titles and abstracts). December 2024: selection of eligible studies (full-text reading) and data extraction. January 2025: data synthesis and analysis. February 2025: manuscript writing and consultation with experts. March 2025: final revision, submission for publication and dissemination of results. supplementary material online supplemental file 1 bmjopen-14-12-s001.docx (13.8KB, docx) DOI: 10.1136/bmjopen-2024-090463 Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the librarian Zélia from the Faculty of Medicine of São José do Rio Preto - FAMERP, for her assistance in developing the search strategy and accessing the databases. Footnotes Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Prepublication history and additional supplemental material for this paper are available online. To view these files, please visit the journal online ( https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2024-090463 ). Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer-reviewed. Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were not involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Contributor Information Emerson Roberto dos Santos, Email: emerson.santos@edu.famerp.br. Aline Russomano de Gouvêa, Email: alinerussomanogouvea@gmail.com. Natália Almeida de Arnaldo Silva Rodriguez Castro, Email: natalia.castro@edu.famerp.br. João Daniel de Souza Menezes, Email: joao.menezes@edu.famerp.br. Matheus Querino da Silva, Email: matheusquirino@hotmail.com. Helena Landin Gonçalves Cristóvão, Email: helena.cristovao@edu.famerp.br. Cíntia Canato Martins, Email: cintiacanato1@gmail.com. Jéssica Gisleine de Oliveira, Email: jessica.gisleine@edu.famerp.br. Patrícia da Silva Fucuta, Email: patricia@fukuta.com.br. Alexandre Lins Werneck, Email: alexandrelinswerneck@gmail.com. Gerardo Maria de Araújo Filho, Email: gerardo.filho@famerp.br. Alba Regina de Abreu Lima, Email: alba.lima09@gmail.com. Heloisa Cristina Caldas, Email: heloisa@famerp.br. Vânia Maria Sabadoto Brienze, Email: vania.brienze@hospitaldebase.com.br. Júlio César André, Email: julio.andre@edu.famerp.br. Antônio Hélio Oliani, Email: oliani@famerp.br. References 1. Costa Figueiredo M, Huynh T, Takei A, et al. Goals, life events, and transitions: examining fertility apps for holistic health tracking. JAMIA Open. 2021;4:ooab013. doi: 10.1093/jamiaopen/ooab013. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Curchoe CL. Smartphone Applications for Reproduction: From Rigorously Validated and Clinically Relevant to Potentially Harmful. EMJ Repro Health. 2020:85–91. doi: 10.33590/emjreprohealth/20-00033. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Vander Borght M, Wyns C. Fertility and infertility: Definition and epidemiology. Clin Biochem. 2018;62:2–10. doi: 10.1016/j.clinbiochem.2018.03.012. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Rooney KL, Domar AD. The relationship between stress and infertility. Dialogues Clin Neurosci. 2018;20:41–7. doi: 10.31887/DCNS.2018.20.1/klrooney. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Ford EA, Peters AE, Roman SD, et al. A scoping review of the information provided by fertility smartphone applications. Hum Fertil (Camb) 2022;25:625–39. doi: 10.1080/14647273.2021.1871784. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Ford EA, Roman SD, McLaughlin EA, et al. The association between reproductive health smartphone applications and fertility knowledge of Australian women. BMC Womens Health. 2020;20:45. doi: 10.1186/s12905-020-00912-y. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hohmann‐Marriott B, Williams T, Girling J. Fertility and infertility uses of menstrual apps from the perspectives of healthcare providers and patients. Aust NZ J Obst Gynaeco . 2024;64:252–7. doi: 10.1111/ajo.13781. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Kruglova K, O’Connell SBL, Dawadi S, et al. An mHealth App to Support Fertility Patients Navigating the World of Infertility (Infotility): Development and Usability Study. JMIR Form Res . 2021;5:e28136. 