ls in patients with chronic autoimmune thyroiditis: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Thyroid. 2016;26(12):1681–1692. doi: 10.1089/thy.2016.0256. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Winther KH, Rayman MP, Bonnema SJ, Hegedüs L. Selenium in thyroid disorders - essential knowledge for clinicians. Nat Rev Endocrinol. 2020;16(3):165–176. doi: 10.1038/s41574-019-0311-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Survey 1 Thyroid Working Group of the Turkish Society for Pediatric Endocrinology and Diabetes https://d2v96fxpocvxx.cloudfront.net/32e1d65a-7ef3-4d28-858e-7da63d316173/content-images/fc1f34aa-f3db-4ce3-95fb-f273aa611737.pdf',2,'2025-10-09 07:36:52','2025-10-09 07:36:52');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(897,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11322620','Standardized Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility Consultation for Pediatric and Adolescent Oncology Patients.','Hartup LA, Go VA, Robinson RD.','2024','postpubertal gonadotoxic elections elections elections automates ambivalent 20220154ex gonadotoxic postpubertal gonadotoxic gonadotoxic nonalkylating hippa carelink carelink bexar histiocytosis hemangioendothelioma carelink elections carelink carelink carelink carelink nonalkylating nonalkylating nonalkylating nonalkylating preselected ambivalent infeasible prepubescent reimplanting ejaculate elections recency endorsements nonalkylating gonadotoxic electing anaheim bleyer ries gurney npcr letourneau ebbel bahadur s0303 victorson choy jayao oktay assanasen knudtson jayao vadaparampil moerdler duvall lattin jayao donen klosky meacham e325 e333 frisch e153 gonadotoxic dolmans donnez cacciottola transplanting molmed dongarwar adu gyamfi noncent voigt blakemore mcculloh fino s10815 howards balthazar mersereau des133 gonadotoxic gonadectomy','Abstract Purpose: To investigate the role of formal reproductive endocrinology and infertility (REI) consultation in fertility preservation counseling in a pediatric/adolescent oncology patient population. Methods: Retrospective chart review was performed at an academic adult hospital from 2021 to 2022. Pre- and postpubertal patients admitted to the pediatric/adolescent oncology service with cancer diagnoses and imminent gonadotoxic chemotherapy plans were included. Baseline characteristics were collected, including patient age, sex, race, language, insurance, and cancer diagnosis. Primary outcomes were formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election. Results: Nineteen of 58 eligible patients received a formal REI consultation. Patients were more likely to elect fertility preservation if they received a consult. Females were more likely to receive a consult than males and more likely to elect fertility preservation. Patients of age ≥16 years were more likely to receive consultation than younger patients. However, all patients of age <16 years who received a consult elected fertility preservation. There was no difference in consultation based on race, language, or insurance. Thirteen of 19 patients who received an REI consultation elected fertility preservation. Ten of 11 female elections were ovarian suppression, an unproven method of fertility preservation. The two male elections were semen cryopreservation. Conclusion: Underutilization of formal REI consults and a relative lack of proven fertility preservation elections may shed light on a need for increased fertility preservation awareness among young oncology patients and the providers who care for them. A streamlined process that automates formal REI consultation for all eligible patients may maximize the potential for comprehensive counseling and improve patient participation in fertility preservation. Keywords: fertility preservation, cryopreservation, pediatric, adolescent, reproductive endocrinology, infertility Introduction Pediatric and adolescent cancer incidence has increased over the past 75 years. 1 , 2 According to the CDC, approximately 15,000 children and adolescents younger than 20 and 21,000 young adults of age 20–30 years are diagnosed with cancer each year in the United States. 3 With advancements in treatment, pediatric cancer death rates have decreased almost 70% over the past 40 years. 3 Consequently, greater emphasis has been placed on the long-term effects of treatment on the quality of life for these patients. At the forefront of this discussion is the potential compromise to future fertility. Fertility preservation is highly valued by patients. 4–6 Fertility preservation counseling is associated with higher patient satisfaction and has been found to result in less decision regret, regardless of the decision to elect or decline treatment personally. 4 One study found that even just knowing fertility preservation options are available provides a sense of reassurance to the patient regarding the future. 5 On the contrary, failure to discuss fertility preservation options can result in both patient and parent regret, potentially resulting in legal settlements. 5 , 6 The importance of fertility preservation is highlighted by the American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO) releasing clinical practice guidelines for fertility preservation counseling and treatment. These guidelines were first released in 2006 and have since been updated in 2013 and 2018. The first recommendation states that health care providers caring for adult and pediatric patients with cancer should address the possibility of infertility as soon as possible before treatment starts, and that patients should be referred to reproductive specialists if they express an interest in or are ambivalent to fertility preservation. 7 Despite the establishment of these guidelines, there has not been universal implementation into clinical practice. Three years after the first update, a prior survey study found fewer than 35% of pediatric oncologists were familiar with the ASCO guidelines, and only 28% referred patients to reproductive endocrinology and infertility (REI) for fertility preservation. 8 Another study that surveyed 613 physicians across various institutions and practice settings found that only 47% of oncologists typically refer patients to REI. 9 An anonymous cross-sectional survey study found that 85% of pediatric oncology program directors reported lacking formal adolescent and young adult (AYA) curricula. 10 In addition, 36% reported little to no education in oncofertility, and 42% reported little to no education in survivorship. 10 In response to these concerns, our center implemented a protocol to emphasize fertility preservation counseling by the pediatric oncology service. A grant was also received to ensure that patients are offered REI referral, with navigators and social workers available for facilitation if desired. The question remains as to which patients receive a formal REI referral versus which do not. Some studies have found providers report being less willing to offer fertility preservation to patients with a low potential for fertility or cure and to families of lower socioeconomic status. 11 In prepubertal patients, there is the added barrier of the only viable fertility preservation option being tissue cryopreservation. Ovarian cryopreservation was previously considered experimental and testicular cryopreservation is still considered experimental. 12 The purpose of this study was to investigate the role of formal REI consultation for fertility preservation counseling in a pediatric and adolescent oncology patient population. Specifically, we wanted to assess whether certain patient demographic factors are associated with increased REI referral and whether an REI referral is associated with increased utilization of fertility preservation. Methods Institutional Review Board approval was obtained on April 4, 2022, under Protocol 20220154EX. Study population Patients admitted to the pediatric/adolescent oncology service at an academic adult hospital between January 2021 and March 2022 were identified. Those with cancer diagnoses and plans to undergo imminent gonadotoxic chemotherapy were included. Both pre- and postpubertal patients were included. Patients up to the age of 39 years were included if their diagnosis was considered a pediatric or adolescent cancer and they had not yet initiated treatment. The upper age limit was chosen in keeping with that defined by the National Cancer Institute. Prepubertal patients were included as our institution can provide all types of fertility preservation, including tissue cryopreservation. Patients with plans to undergo radiation alone were excluded owing to the varying effects on fertility depending on the anticipated location of treatment. Institutional processes At our institution, fertility preservation counseling is routinely provided to all patients by members of the primary care team—either pediatric/adolescent oncology or adult oncology. This counseling can be performed by different members of the team, including physicians, nursing staff, and/or social workers. Counseling includes a discussion of the risks of gonadotoxic chemotherapy to future fertility and fertility preservation options available, including semen cryopreservation, oocyte cryopreservation, embryo cryopreservation, ovarian tissue cryopreservation, or gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) ovarian suppression. Patients are counseled as to the experimental nature of GnRH agonists for fertility preservation. At our institution, all fertility preservation options are provided by REI, and they are available to prepubertal patients. Referrals can be made to REI for any patient with plans to undergo gonadotoxic cancer treatment, regardless of whether the patient plans to proceed with fertility preservation, but the decision to refer is made by the primary care team. REI is available for consultation in both the outpatient and inpatient settings, including at the time of cancer diagnosis. There are no differences in the fertility preservation counseling process based on the urgency of chemotherapy start. Semen cryopreservation is provided by the REI service. Although referrals can be made to urology for counseling regarding semen cryopreservation, the service is provided by REI. We do not have a pediatric and adolescent gynecology division at our institution, and general surgery does not perform tissue cryopreservation. We do not currently offer testicular cryopreservation at our institution, as this treatment is still considered experimental. Study design Retrospective chart review was conducted from April to May 2022. Baseline characteristics were collected, including patient age, sex, race, primary language, insurance type, and cancer diagnosis. The planned chemotherapy regimen was also noted to be alkylating or nonalkylating. Primary outcomes were documentation of formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election. Formal REI consultation was defined as an electronic medical record referral order to REI in the patient’s chart or documentation by an REI provider in the patient’s chart that counseling had been performed by REI. Fertility preservation election was defined as semen cryopreservation, oocyte cryopreservation, embryo cryopreservation, ovarian tissue cryopreservation, or GnRH ovarian suppression. The time line of diagnosis, consult, and chemotherapy was also assessed. Data analysis All data were deidentified, entered, and stored in a secure, Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPPA)-compliant web-based database. Prism was utilized to perform statistical analysis. Categorical data were evaluated using Fisher’s exact test. Odds ratios (ORs) and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated. A p value of <0.05 was determined to be significant. Results A total of 58 patients met the inclusion criteria, of which 35 were male and 23 were female. Ages ranged from 2.5 to 39 years, with a mean age of 17.6. Twelve patients were prepubertal. Only five patients were older than 25. Of the included patients, 12 were Caucasian, 45 were Hispanic, and one was African American. Insurance coverage included private ( n = 26), Medicaid ( n = 17), CareLink ( n = 11), or none ( n = 4). CareLink is a financial assistance program offered to Bexar County residents who do not have health insurance or qualify for other coverage. The primary languages spoken by the patients were English ( n = 45) and Spanish ( n = 13). Patient diagnoses can be seen in Table 1 . The most common diagnoses were acute lymphoblastic leukemia ( n = 15), osteosarcoma ( n = 8), and testicular cancer ( n = 6). Primary care teams included pediatric oncology and adult oncology. Table 1. Baseline Patient Characteristics Percentage of Patients ( n ) Sex Male 60% (35) Female 40% (23) Age (years) 0–5 12% (7) 6–10 9% (5) 11–15 10% (6) 16–20 29% (17) 21–25 31% (18) 26–30 7% (4) 31–40 2% (1) Diagnosis Acute lymphoblastic leukemia 26% (15) Osteosarcoma 14% (8) Testicular cancer 10% (6) Ewing sarcoma 7% (4) Glioma 7% (4) Large B cell lymphoma 5% (3) Hodgkin lymphoma 3% (2) Mesenchymal chondrosarcoma 3% (2) Rhabdomyosarcoma 3% (2) Medulloblastoma 2% (1) Astrocytoma 2% (1) Wilms tumor 2% (1) Giant cell granuloma 2% (1) Langerhans cell histiocytosis 2% (1) Desmoplastic round cell tumor 2% (1) Hemangioendothelioma 2% (1) Gastrointestinal neuroectodermal tumor 2% (1) Anaplastic sarcoma of kidney 2% (1) Synovial sarcoma 2% (1) Prostate sarcoma 2% (1) Sarcoma, unspecified 2% (1) Insurance Private 45% (26) Medicaid 29% (17) CareLink 19% (11) None 7% (4) Language English 78% (45) Spanish 22% (13) Race Caucasian 21% (12) Hispanic 78% (45) African American 2% (1) Open in a new tab Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election It is the practice of the pediatric and adult oncology divisions at our institution that all eligible patients are counseled by members of their respective teams regarding fertility preservation. Of the 58 eligible patients, 19 (33%) received a formal REI consult for fertility preservation. The mean time from cancer diagnosis to REI consultation was 1.9 days. The mean time from REI consultation to start of chemotherapy was 3.8 days. Of the 19 patients who received a consult, 13 elected fertility preservation. In the absence of a formal REI consultation, two patients elected fertility preservation, while 37 patients did not. Patients were more likely to proceed with fertility preservation if they received a formal REI consult than if there was no documented consult (OR, 40.1; 95% CI [7.32, 192]; p < 0.01). Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election by patient sex Of the 19 patients who received a formal REI consultation, 12 were female and 7 were male. Males were less likely to receive a formal REI consult than females (OR, 0.23; 95% CI [0.07, 0.74]; p < 0.05) and less likely to elect fertility preservation after consultation (OR, 0.04; 95% CI [0.003, 0.46]; p < 0.01) ( Table 2 ). Of the 23 females in the study, 11 elected fertility preservation. Ten (91%) of 11 female elections were ovarian suppression with GnRH agonists, and 1 was oocyte cryopreservation. Of the 35 males in the study, 2 elected fertility preservation. Both chose semen cryopreservation. Table 2. Formal REI Consultation and Fertility Preservation Election by Patient Sex Male ( n = 35) Female ( n = 23) OR; 95% CI; p value REI consultation 7/35 a 12/23 a 0.23; [0.07, 0.74]; p < 0.05 Fertility preservation election 2/7 b 11/12 b 0.04; [0.003, 0.46]; p < 0.01 Open in a new tab a Ratio of patients who had an EMR order placed for REI consultation from the entire sample size. b Ratio of patients who elected fertility preservation after receiving an REI consultation. EMR, electronic medical record; REI, reproductive endocrinology and infertility; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election by patient age Two patients younger than 16 received a formal REI consult for fertility preservation, while 16 patients did not. The ages of the two patients who received an REI consultation were 14 and 15 years. No prepubertal patients received a formal REI consultation. The ages of the patients of age <16 years who did not receive a consult ranged from 2.5 to 14, with a mean age of nine. In patients of age ≥16 years, a total of 17 patients received an REI consult, while 23 patients did not. Patients of age <16 years were less likely to receive a consult than those ≥16 years (OR, 0.17; 95% CI [0.04, 0.72]; p < 0.05) ( Table 3 ). Of the 17 patients who were ≥16 years who received a formal consult, 11 elected fertility preservation. All patients of age <16 years who received a formal consult elected fertility preservation. Table 3. Formal REI Consultation and Fertility Preservation Election by Patient Age 0–15 years ( n = 18) ≥16 years ( n = 40) OR; 95% CI; p value REI consultation 2/18 a 17/40 a 0.17; [0.04, 0.72]; p < 0.05 Fertility preservation election 2/2 b 11/17 b + infinity; [0.21, + infinity]; p = 0.30 Open in a new tab a Ratio of patients who had an EMR order placed for REI consultation from the entire sample size. b Ratio of patients who elected fertility preservation after receiving an REI consultation. EMR, electronic medical record; REI, reproductive endocrinology and infertility; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election by patient insurance Ten of 26 patients with private insurance received a formal REI consult for fertility preservation. Five of 17 patients with Medicaid received an REI consultation. There was no difference in the number of patients receiving an REI consult in privately insured patients compared with patients with Medicaid (OR, 1.50; 95% CI [0.42, 5.32]; p = 0.74) ( Table 4 ). There was no difference in the consultation rate of privately insured patients compared with patients with CareLink (OR, 1.09; 95% CI [0.29, 4.01]; p > 0.99) or no insurance (OR + infinity; 95% CI [0.45, + infinity]; p = 0.27). Of the 10 patients with private insurance who received an REI consult, 9 elected fertility preservation. In the five patients with Medicaid who received an REI consult, one elected fertility preservation. Privately insured patients were more likely to elect fertility preservation than patients with Medicaid (OR, 36.0; 95% CI [1.5, 468]; p < 0.01). No difference was seen in the fertility preservation election rate between those with private insurance versus CareLink (OR, 3.00; 95% [0.12, 62.2]; p = 0.51) ( Table 4 ). Table 4. Formal REI Consultation and Fertility Preservation Election by Patient Insurance Private ( n = 26) Other ( n = 32) OR; 95% CI; p value REI consultation Private 10/26 a Medicaid 5/17 a CareLink 4/11 a None 0/4 a 1.50; [0.42, 5.32]; p = 0.74 1.09; [0.29, 4.01]; p > 0.99 + infinity; [0.45, + infinity]; p = 0.27 Fertility preservation election Private 9/10 b Medicaid 1/5 b CareLink 3/4 b 36.0; [1.50, 468]; p < 0.05 3.00; [0.12, 62.2]; p = 0.51 Open in a new tab a Ratio of patients who had an EMR order placed for REI consultation from the entire sample size. b Ratio of patients who elected fertility preservation after receiving an REI consultation. EMR, electronic medical record; REI, reproductive endocrinology and infertility; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election by patient race Fifteen of 45 Hispanic patients received a formal REI consultation. Of the 12 Caucasian patients, 3 received an REI consultation. The one African American patient received an REI consult. There was no difference in REI consultation rate by patient race (OR, 0.63; 95% CI [0.17, 2.49]; p = 0.73) ( Table 5 ). Of the patients who elected fertility preservation, 10 were Hispanic, 2 were Caucasian, and 1 was African American. There was no difference in fertility preservation election based on race (OR, 0.91; 95% CI [0.09, 15.6]; p > 0.99) ( Table 5 ). Table 5. Formal REI Consultation and Fertility Preservation Election by Patient Race Caucasian ( n = 12) Hispanic or African American ( n = 45 + 1) OR; 95% CI; p value REI consultation 3/12 a 16/46 a 0.63; [0.17, 2.49]; p = 0.73 Fertility preservation election 2/3 b 11/16 b 0.91; [0.09, 15.6]; p > 0.99 Open in a new tab a Ratio of patients who had an EMR order placed for REI consultation from the entire sample size. b Ratio of patients who elected fertility preservation after receiving an REI consultation. EMR, electronic medical record; REI, reproductive endocrinology and infertility; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election by patient language Of the 45 patients whose primary language was English, 13 received a formal REI consult. Six of 13 Spanish-speaking patients received a formal REI consultation. There was no difference in the rate of REI consultation based on primary language (OR, 0.47; 95% CI [0.13, 1.63]; p = 0.32). Of the patients who elected fertility preservation, eight spoke English, while five spoke Spanish. There was no difference in fertility preservation election based on primary language (OR, 0.32; 95% CI [0.02, 3.15]; p = 0.60). Formal REI consultation and fertility preservation election by planned chemotherapy A total of 50 patients had plans to undergo an alkylating chemotherapy regimen, while 8 patients had plans to undergo a nonalkylating regimen. Seventeen of 50 patients in the alkylating group received an REI consult compared with 2 of 8 patients in the nonalkylating group. There was no difference in the rate of REI consultation based on planned chemotherapy (OR, 1.55; 95% CI [0.32, 8.11]; p = 0.61). Thirteen of 50 patients in the alkylating group elected fertility preservation compared with 2 of 8 patients in the nonalkylating group. There was no difference in the rate of fertility preservation election based on the type of chemotherapy planned (OR, 1.05; 95% CI [0.21, 5.64]; p = 0.95) ( Table 6 ). Table 6. Formal REI Consultation and Fertility Preservation Election by Planned Chemotherapy Alkylating Nonalkylating OR; 95% CI; p value REI consultation 17/50 a 2/8 a 1.55; [0.32, 8.11]; p = 0.61 Fertility preservation election 13/50 b 2/8 b 1.05; [0.21, 5.64]; p = 0.95 Open in a new tab a Ratio of patients who had an EMR order placed for REI consultation from the entire sample size. b Ratio of patients who elected fertility preservation after receiving an REI consultation. EMR, electronic medical record; REI, reproductive endocrinology and infertility; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. Discussion In this study, we found that patients who received a formal REI consultation were more likely to elect fertility preservation than patients who did not receive a consultation. It is possible that the processes at our institution preselected this group by fertility preservation discussions already had with the pediatric and adult oncology teams. However, only 33% of eligible patients at our institution received a formal REI consultation. We found this proportion noteworthy in light of the ASCO guidelines, which recommend referral to reproductive specialists for any patients who express an interest in or are ambivalent to fertility preservation. 7 While we did not assess patient attitudes, it seems unlikely that 67% of patients would decline further discussion with a reproductive specialist given the known value of fertility preservation to patients. 4–6 It is also unlikely that the paucity of formal REI referrals is explained by a lack of familiarity with the ASCO guidelines, as this has been an emphasis of the oncology divisions at our institutions, as described in the Introduction section. Other investigators have reported similarly low rates of referral to REI for fertility preservation counseling. A recent study at the Cleveland Clinic reported that only 15.4% of patients with newly diagnosed gynecologic cancers were referred to REI by their gynecologic oncologist. 13 This study found similarly high rates (44.1%) of fertility preservation election in the referred group. 13 Other notable findings in our study included the association found with patient demographic factors and REI consultation rate. While we did not find an association with patient race, language, or insurance status, we did identify associations with sex and age. Females were more likely to receive a formal REI consult than males, and they were more likely to elect fertility preservation. To the best of our knowledge, no other studies have identified this difference. This could be explained by our institutional practice, where male patients are frequently referred to urology for fertility preservation, although semen cryopreservation is performed through our REI clinic. It is also possible that physician perception played a role. One study found that REI referrals are more likely to be made by female physicians and those with favorable attitudes. 9 There may be a perception that future fertility is more important to females than males. The reason for the higher election rate among females compared with males is unclear outside of the higher formal REI consultation rate. Notably, 10 of 11 females in this study elected a GnRH agonist for fertility preservation. There is conflicting evidence whether to recommend GnRH agonists given their lack of proven efficacy. The ASCO guidelines state that GnRH agonists should not be used in place of proven fertility preservation methods, such as oocyte, embryo, or tissue cryopreservation. 7 However, when proven fertility preservation methods are unavailable, not feasible, or in the setting of young women with breast cancer, GnRH agonists may be offered. 7 In the present study, with such a rapid time line from cancer diagnosis to start of treatment, oocyte, embryo, or tissue cryopreservation was likely infeasible for most patients. Cost also may have played a role and has been cited as a barrier. 12 It is also possible that patients who selected GnRH agonists were at a lower risk for infertility, and the risks and costs of proven fertility preservation methods were not justified in these cases. A future survey that assessed why patients chose their election over other options could be performed to assess this further. The other piece of patient demographic data that correlated with the REI consultation rate in this study was patient age. No prepubertal patients received a formal REI consult, and no prepubertal patients elected fertility preservation. This likely was a result of the only fertility preservation options for prepubescent patients being ovarian and testicular cryopreservation. Ovarian cryopreservation was previously considered experimental until December 2019, and testicular cryopreservation is still considered experimental. 14 Although now mitigated, concerns were previously raised about the risk of reimplanting malignant cells through these procedures. 15 Tissue cryopreservation is invasive, and, for many patients, the decision to pursue this treatment would need to be made by the patient’s parents, raising potential ethical concerns. Furthermore, because this option could only recently be offered for female patients at our institution, the primary team may have perceived that it was not yet available for patients. Because we do not currently offer testicular cryopreservation at our institution, male fertility preservation options are limited to semen cryopreservation, which requires the ability to produce an ejaculate. This likely explains the lower number of REI referrals and fertility preservation elections in prepubertal males in our population. Although no statistically significant associations were found between a formal REI consultation and race, this study was likely underpowered to evaluate for this association if it was present. Prior large retrospective cohort studies have found African American and Hispanic women are 70% less likely to receive fertility treatment than Caucasian women. 16 Other studies have reported that African American patients are less likely to receive counseling for fertility preservation by oncologists 17 and less likely to seek fertility preservation after a cancer diagnosis. 18 There has been limited research regarding referral patterns in fertility care. Given the known racial disparities that exist within reproductive health care, 19 this possibility for REI referral bias warrants further investigation in a larger study. Similarly, although we did not find any statistically significant associations between a formal REI consultation and insurance status, this study was likely underpowered to assess for this association if it was present. Fertility preservation procedures are expensive, and cost is frequently cited as a barrier for patients. 12 Prior studies have reported that patients with insurance are significantly more likely to receive fertility preservation counseling and consultation than patients without an insurance. 20–21 This possibility should be further investigated in a larger sample size, as differences in REI consultation based on insurance would lead to further disparities in fertility preservation counseling. The strengths of the present study include recency of data and a single academic hospital location with an on-site, robust REI division capable of providing all types of fertility preservation and oncology providers who have embraced the ASCO guidelines. Other similar studies date back further, before the release of the updated ASCO guidelines and American Society of Reproductive Medicine (ASRM) endorsements in fertility preservation. Ovarian tissue cryopreservation is not considered experimental anymore and is supported by the ASRM. 22 Yet, no patients chose this option at our institution, raising the question of why. As reported at many centers, cost may be the most prohibitive reason, as the laparoscopy required to obtain the gonadal tissue is not routinely covered by insurance. The main limitations of this study stem from its methodology and small numbers of patient charts available for review. This study did not ensure that all mechanisms of REI referral were captured, such as those made via email or telephone. In addition, with only inpatients examined, the present study does not account for the discussions had in the outpatient setting, described in another electronic medical record, or had with other members of the health care team, such as nurses or social workers. It is possible and likely at our institution that patients who did not receive a formal REI consult were counseled regarding fertility preservation, but the patient or family was not interested in fertility preservation. If such conversations were previously had, the inpatient oncology provider may have made the decision to defer REI consultation. A potential follow-up study could be conducted to assess fertility preservation election rates after implementation of a formal automatic REI referral system, accounting for outpatient and informal REI referrals separate from the control group. The study’s retrospective design is another limitation, which is subject to bias and allows only correlations, not causation, to be identified. Although associations with demographic data, such as patient sex and age were found, other variables may have confounded these results, including the type of cancer patients were diagnosed with and their prognoses. Although we did not find a difference in the REI consultation rate or fertility preservation election rate based on chemotherapy being alkylating or nonalkylating, there may have been a difference based on the risk of the proposed chemotherapy regimen causing infertility, which was not examined in this study. In addition, physician attitudes and practice patterns may have influenced which patients received REI consultation. We did not examine physician attitudes in this study, but this could be examined in the future. In addition, patients could be surveyed to assess what conversations were had with their physician regarding fertility preservation and if they were specifically offered an REI consult. Furthermore, the positive association seen between REI consultation and fertility preservation election may be explained by the patients who already knew they desired fertility preservation being the same individuals who received REI consultation. One study found that a key predictor of REI referral was having a patient who asked about fertility preservation. 8 This could be further elucidated by reviewing all forms of clinical documentation from other members of the clinical team, as stated above. Based on the findings of our study and the known regret associated with not receiving fertility preservation counseling, 4–6 we believe formal REI referrals should be made immediately and automatically for all patients with cancer who had plans to undergo gonadotoxic chemotherapy with the option to opt out if a patient declines. Based on our current numbers of new cancer diagnoses each year, this is feasible at our institution. However, at institutions where there are higher numbers of new cancer diagnoses, it may be more reasonable for an automatic formal REI referral to be in place when the proposed treatment has high or intermediate risk of compromising future fertility. One study found that survivors of childhood cancer reported that they wished a reproductive specialist would have been part of the conversation surrounding fertility preservation at the time of their cancer diagnosis. 6 Existing attitudes support this multidisciplinary approach. From the patient and parent perspective, hearing about fertility preservation from multiple physicians with different perspectives at different times would provide further opportunities to ask questions and improve patient knowledge regarding fertility preservation options. In turn, this would likely result in more patients electing fertility preservation. Acknowledgments The authors acknowledge the pediatric oncology division for providing access to their list of admitted patients during the selected time that allowed this chart review to take place and for their feedback regarding this article. Authors’ Contributions L.A.H.: conceptualization (equal), formal analysis (equal), investigation (equal), methodology (equal), visualization (lead), and writing—original draft (lead). V.-A.G.: conceptualization (equal), formal analysis (equal), investigation (equal), methodology (equal), visualization (support), and writing—reviewing and editing (support). R.D.R.: conceptualization (support), supervision (lead), validation (lead), and writing—reviewing and editing (support). Author Disclosure Statement None of the authors have a declaration of interest to report. The findings of this study were previously presented at The American Society of Reproductive Medicine (ASRM) Meeting held October 24–26, 2022, in Anaheim, California. Funding Information No funding was received from any industry, institution, or person to complete this study. References 1. Bleyer A, O’Leary M, Barr R, et al. (eds): Cancer Epidemiology in Older Adolescents and Young Adults 15 to 29 Years of Age, Including SEER Incidence and Survival: 1975–2000. National Cancer Institute, NIH Pub. No. 06-5767. Bethesda, MD, 2006. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Ries LAG, Smith MA, Gurney JG, et al. (eds). Cancer Incidence and Survival among Children and Adolescents: United States SEER Program 1975–1995, National Cancer Institute, SEER Program. NIH Pub. No. 99-4649. Bethesda, MD, 1999. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Division of Cancer Prevention and Control. Tracking pediatric and young adult cancer cases. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. 2022. Available from: https://www.cdc.gov/cancer/npcr/pediatric-young-adult-cancer.htm [Last accessed: February 21, 2023]. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Letourneau JM, Ebbel EE, Katz PP, et al. Pretreatment fertility counseling and fertility preservation improve quality of life in reproductive age women with cancer. Cancer 2012;118(6):1710–1717; doi: 10.1002/cncr.26459 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Bahadur G. Fertility issues for cancer patients. Mol Cell Endocrinol 2000;169(1–2):117–122; doi: 10.1016/s0303-7207(00)00364-6 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Stein DM, Victorson DE, Choy JT, et al. Fertility preservation preferences and perspectives among adult male survivors of pediatric cancer and their parents. J Adolesc Young Adult Oncol 2014;3(2):75–82; doi: 10.1089/jayao.2014.0007 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Oktay K, Harvey BE, Partridge AH, et al. Fertility preservation in patients with cancer: ASCO clinical practice guideline update. J Clin Oncol 2018;36(19):1994–2001; doi: 10.1200/JCO.2018.78.1914 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Campbell JE, Assanasen C, Robinson RD, Knudtson JF. Fertility preservation counseling for pediatric and adolescent cancer patients. J Adolesc Young Adult Oncol 2016;5(1):58–63; doi: 10.1089/jayao.2015.0040 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Quinn GP, Vadaparampil ST, Lee JH, et al. Physician referral for fertility preservation in oncology patients: A national study of practice behaviors. J Clin Oncol 2009;27(35):5952–5957; doi: 10.1200/JCO.2009.23.0250 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Moerdler S, DuVall AS, Hayes-Lattin B, et al. Gaps in adolescent and young adult cancer education in oncology fellowship training. J Adolesc Young Adult Oncol 2023;13(1):97–104; doi: 10.1089/jayao.2023.0056 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Gupta AA, Donen RM, Sung L, et al. Testicular biopsy for fertility preservation in prepubertal boys with cancer: Identifying preferences for procedure and reactions to disclosure practices. J Urol 2016;196(1):219–224; doi: 10.1016/j.juro.2016.02.2967 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Frederick NN, Klosky JL, Meacham LR, et al. Infrastructure of fertility preservation services for pediatric cancer patients: A report from the children’s oncology group. JCO Oncol Pract 2022;18(3):e325–e333; doi: 10.1200/OP.21.00275 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Frisch E, Chau D, Neumann O, et al. Window of opportunity: Rate of referral to infertility providers among reproductive age women with newly diagnosed gynecologic cancers. Fertility and Sterility 2023;120(4):e153; doi: 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2023.08.464 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Ethics Committee of American Society for Reproductive Medicine. Fertility preservation and reproduction in patients facing gonadotoxic therapies: A committee opinion. Fertil Steril 2013;100(5):1224–1231; doi: 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2013.08.041 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Dolmans MM, Donnez J, Cacciottola L. Fertility preservation: The challenge of freezing and transplanting ovarian tissue. Trends Mol Med 2021;27(8):777–791; doi: 10.1016/j.molmed.2020.11.003 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Dongarwar D, Mercado-Evans V, Adu-Gyamfi S, et al. Racial/ethnic disparities in infertility treatment utilization in the US, 2011–2019. Syst Biol Reprod Med 2022;68(3):180–189; doi: 10.1080/19396368.2022.2038718 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Lawson AK, McGuire JM, Noncent E, et al. Disparities in counseling female cancer patients for fertility preservation. J Womens Health 2017;26(8):886–891; doi: 10.1089/jwh.2016.5997 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Voigt PE, Blakemore JK, McCulloh D, Fino ME. Equal opportunity for all? An analysis of race and ethnicity in fertility preservation in New York City. J Assist Reprod Genet 2020;37(12):3095–3102; doi: 10.1007/s10815-020-01980-7 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Weiss MS, Marsh EE. Navigating unequal paths: Racial disparities in the infertility journey. Obstet Gynecol 2023;142(4):940–947; doi: 10.1097/AOG.0000000000005354 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Chin HB, Howards PP, Kramer MR, et al. Which female cancer patients fail to receive fertility counseling before treatment in the state of Georgia? Fertil Steril 2016;106(7):1763–1771.e1; doi: 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2016.08.034 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Goodman LR, Balthazar U, Kim J, Mersereau JE. Trends of socioeconomic disparities in referral patterns for fertility preservation consultation. Hum Reprod 2012;27(7):2076–2081; doi: 10.1093/humrep/des133 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Practice Committee of the American Society for reproductive medicine. Fertility preservation in patients undergoing gonadotoxic therapy or gonadectomy: A committee opinion. Fertil Steril 2019;112(6):1022–1033; doi: 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2019.09.013 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',216,'2025-10-09 07:37:02','2025-10-09 07:37:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(898,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11339204','Outcomes of oocyte retrievals completed by reproductive endocrinology and infertility fellows versus faculty: a 7-year retrospective review.','Aase DA, Miller C, Weaver A, Li Y, Zhao Y, Babayev SN.','2024','chronologically s10815 sart aspirating insemination insemination intracytoplasmic aspirating chronologically chronologically orrs orrs chronologically insemination coflare luteal insemination chronologically chronologically chronologically orrs chronologically chronologically cusum cusum dessolle cusum verhaeghe cusum cusum verhaeghe soave aspirating babayev babayev aase aase babayev aase babayev aase artdata polyzos drakopoulos pellicer tournaye intracytoplasmic panayotidis amso marci matorras onofriescu hoz025 yauger payson segars stegmann dessolle leperlier biau freour barriere rbmo verhaeghe hachem inchboard corroenne dreux jeanneteau ir_faqs soave piva marci s10916 corroenne legendre riethmuller hachem dreux pickup pickupsim','Abstract Purpose To examine outcomes of oocyte retrievals completed by Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility (REI) fellows versus faculty physicians. Methods This retrospective cohort study examined patients who underwent oocyte retrievals at Mayo Clinic from July 15, 2009, to December 15, 2016. The primary outcome was the oocyte retrieval rate (ORR) calculated per retrieval as the number of oocytes retrieved per follicles aspirated. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test was used to compare follicle and oocyte counts and ORR between fellows and faculty during the same bilateral retrieval. Results The study cohort included the first bilateral retrieval from 845 unique patients completed by 11 fellows and seven faculty. The median ORR was not statistically different for fellows and faculty (0.79 versus 0.80, p = 0.46). To assess for a learning curve, the outcomes of seven fellows who completed at least 80 retrievals in their first year were examined as four chronologically ordered sets of 20. When these sets were compared to the faculty physician mean ORR, no significant differences were found ( p -values of 0.69, 0.69, 0.81, and 0.81, respectively). Conclusion There were no significant differences in oocyte retrieval rates between fellows versus faculty over a 7-year period, with no significant learning curve observed. These findings suggest that fellows possess the requisite skills for successful oocyte retrieval upon entering REI fellowship following their OB/GYN residency. However, this does not diminish the critical role of comprehensive fellowship training and close supervision, especially in initial and complex cases. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s10815-024-03177-8. Keywords: Oocyte retrieval, Oocyte aspiration, Fellow, Fellowship, Learning curve Introduction The use of assisted reproductive technologies (ART) has increased over the past decade, with approximately 2.0% of all infants born in the United States conceived using ART. In 2019, there were 330,773 ART cycles performed in the United States, resulting in 83,946 live-born infants [ 1 ]. Retrieving an adequate number of mature oocytes is a crucial step of in vitro fertilization (IVF). Previous studies have shown that live birth rates are directly correlated with the number of oocytes retrieved; as the number of oocytes retrieved increases, the cumulative live birth rate also increases [ 2 ]. Oocyte retrieval is, therefore, an important skill for a reproductive endocrinologist to master. Accordingly, Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility (REI) fellowship programs need to train their fellows to perform this skill with expertise. However, the definition of “proficiency” in performing oocyte retrievals has not been well defined. In their latest guidelines published in 2020, the American Society for Reproductive Medicine (ASRM) does not give a minimum number of oocyte retrievals that must be performed. Rather, physicians should have “adequate training to perform diagnostic ultrasound and have performed an adequate number of aspirations under direct supervision to demonstrate proficiency within a practice that meets these standards” [ 3 ]. According to the European Society of Human Reproduction and Embryology (ESHRE) guidelines, a minimum of 30 oocyte retrievals must be performed under supervision to reach clinical competency, although it also notes that “this can vary depending on the type of training, background, and progress of trainees” [ 4 ]. Only a handful of studies have examined the question of “proficiency” in performing oocyte retrievals [ 5 – 7 ]. Those that have looked at this question have published discrepant results, reporting thresholds of 10 retrievals to greater than 50 to reach a competency equivalent to that of faculty physicians. “Competency” is also not a standardized definition, ranging from oocyte yield in 50 to 80% of aspirated follicles. This definition also does not consider other important outcome measures, including postoperative complications, and laboratory and clinical outcomes such as clinical pregnancy and live birth rates. Lastly, small sample sizes have also limited these studies, with the largest study reporting outcomes after 502 fellow oocyte retrievals [ 5 ]. At our institution, fellows perform oocyte retrievals starting at the beginning of their first year of training, allowing us to accrue many retrievals to be examined. Our typical practice is for the REI fellow and the faculty physician to each aspirate one ovary in each patient. Thus, this study aimed to examine the outcomes of oocyte retrievals of individual patients who had one ovary aspirated by an REI fellow and the contralateral ovary aspirated by the faculty physician. The primary hypothesis was that oocyte yield per number of follicles aspirated is similar when the retrieval is completed by either REI fellows or REI faculty physicians. Material and methods The Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board reviewed this retrospective cohort study and determined it to be exempt from the requirement for IRB approval (IRB#: 21–003449). The study cohort consisted of patients aged 18 to 45 who underwent oocyte retrievals for oocyte cryopreservation and/or creation of embryos between July 24, 2009, and December 14, 2016, at Mayo Clinic Rochester. Data were electronically retrieved from a clinical database maintained within the Division of REI at Mayo Clinic. This database is structured to adhere to the required components of the Society for Assisted Reproductive Technology (SART). Patients were included in this study if they had both ovaries in situ at the time of the oocyte retrieval, with one ovary aspirated by an REI fellow and the contralateral ovary aspirated by an REI faculty physician. Patients were excluded if they underwent transabdominal retrieval or declined access to their medical records for research purposes. The time period did not extend beyond 2016 since the number of follicles aspirated was not recorded in our clinical database after 2016. In the United States, training to become an REI physician involves a 4-year residency in general Obstetrics and Gynecology (OB/GYN) followed by a 3-year fellowship in Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility. As defined by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education, a fellowship year begins on July 1 extending through June 30 of the following year [ 8 ]. Typically, most residents do not perform oocyte retrievals and therefore enter fellowship without having previously performed an oocyte retrieval. In our program, fellows were involved in oocyte retrievals under faculty supervision immediately upon the commencement of their fellowship. Additionally, in our program, no minimum number of oocyte retrievals must be observed prior to fellow involvement. Most fellows’ first retrievals occurred within the first 2 weeks of clinical duties during their first year of fellowship. Fellows are involved in oocyte retrievals of all difficulty levels with increased involvement in retrievals that require a higher level of manipulation as their fellowship progresses. During the time frame in which this study was conducted, the goal was to proceed with oocyte retrieval when the patient had at least 4 measurable follicles above 10 mm, with at least 2 follicles measuring at or above 18 mm, and 50% of the follicles measuring 15 mm in size or greater based on transvaginal ultrasound. All visible, non-antral follicles (≥ 10 mm) were aspirated during the retrieval, regardless of the number of follicles seen on the most recent ultrasound. Oocyte retrievals were conducted under transvaginal ultrasound guidance with a needle guide. On the retrieval day, the faculty physician and the fellow chose which ovary they would individually aspirate. Typically, the fellow was the first to choose a side and aspirate. Side-specific follicle counts were determined by the aspirating physician and recorded by the assisting physician in real time. Patient characteristics collected from the clinical and institutional databases included age, BMI, and infertility diagnosis. Clinical characteristics collected included IVF stimulation number, cycle type (donor versus autologous), method of insemination (use of conventional insemination versus intracytoplasmic sperm injection [ICSI]), and IVF medication protocol. The primary outcomes in this study were the number of oocytes retrieved per ovary and the number of follicles aspirated from each ovary. These data were used to calculate the oocyte retrieval rate (ORR) per retrieval per ovary, calculated as the ratio of the number of oocytes retrieved per ovary relative to the number of follicles aspirated from each ovary. If the number of aspirated follicles was 0 then the ORR was set to 0, which occurred in only one retrieval. The number of follicles aspirated was determined during the retrieval by the aspirating physician. It may have been the same or different than the number of expected follicles based on the most recent ultrasound. The statistical analysis for the two study objectives was restricted to the first bilateral retrieval per patient in the time period with one ovary aspirated by a fellow and the contralateral ovary aspirated by a faculty physician. The first objective of this study was to compare these outcomes between REI fellows and REI faculty physicians. Outcomes were compared between the REI fellows and REI faculty in a paired manner using a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test, thereby controlling for any patient-level characteristics. A p -value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. The second objective of this study was to evaluate the ORR of each fellow’s retrievals chronologically over time to assess competency. For each fellow, the retrievals were chronologically ordered from first to last for their first 80 retrievals during their first fellowship year, and the ORR was calculated for each retrieval. Within each ordered set of 20 retrievals, the mean ORR was calculated. The mean ORRs were then graphically displayed over time for each fellow. Fellow competency was defined as performance comparable to faculty physician performance. This definition was selected based on previous papers which have similarly defined trainee competency as compared to faculty physician performance [ 5 , 6 ]. The mean ORRs of the fellows in the chronologically ordered sets of 20 retrievals were compared to the faculty physician mean ORR with a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test. The statistical analysis was performed using SAS software version 9.4 (SAS Institute Inc., Cary, NC). Results A total of 961 patients with research authorization underwent 1137 retrievals during the study period. Among these 1137 retrievals, 1040 were bilateral retrievals with one ovary aspirated by an REI fellow and the contralateral ovary aspirated by an REI faculty physician, of which 1034 had complete data on both the number of follicles aspirated and the number of oocytes retrieved. The following results are based on the first bilateral oocyte retrievals from 845 unique patients over the 7-year period from 2009 to 2016 completed by 11 fellows and seven faculty physicians. Patient characteristics are summarized in Table 1 . The average age at aspiration was 33.1 years. Most included cycles were autologous, with a small number (5.0%) of donor oocyte cycles. The most common primary diagnoses were male factor, ovulatory disorders, and diminished ovarian reserve. The GnRH antagonist was the most used stimulation protocol (69.1%), while ICSI was the most used insemination method (71.5%). Table 1. Patient characteristics at the time of the first bilateral retrieval for 845 unique patients in the study time period Characteristic Total ( N = 845) Age at aspiration (years), mean (SD) 33.1 (4.8) Body mass index (kg/m 2 ), mean (SD) 25.9 (5.5) Cycle type, N (%) Donor 42 (5.0%) Autologous 803 (95.0%) Primary diagnosis Diminished ovarian reserve 108 (12.8%) Male factor 226 (26.7%) Ovulatory disorders (PCOS) 141 (16.7%) Non-infertile reason 27 (3.2%) Unexplained 109 (12.9%) Structural (tubal/uterine) 71 (8.4%) Other 163 (19.3%) Stimulation protocol, N (%) Coflare (GnRH flare) 55 (6.5%) Long luteal (GnRH) 205 (24.3%) GnRH antagonist 584 (69.1%) None 1 (0.1%) Insemination method, N (%) ICSI 604 (71.5%) Standard 237 (28.0%) Standard converted to ICSI 4 (0.4%) Prior fresh embryo transfer, N (%) 0 705 (84.1%) 1 90 (10.7%) 2 20 (2.4%) 3 + 23 (2.7%) Not documented 7 Prior frozen embryo transfer, N (%) 0 804 (95.9%) 1 11 (1.3%) 2 13 (1.6%) 3 + 10 (1.2%) Not documented 7 Open in a new tab For the first study objective, fellows aspirated a higher median follicle number (11 versus 10; p < 0.001) and retrieved more oocytes (8 versus 7; p < 0.001) than faculty. Despite this difference, there was no significant difference in the median ORR between fellows and faculty (0.79 versus 0.80, p = 0.46; Table 2 ). Table 2. Comparison of the number of oocytes retrieved per ovary, number of follicles aspirated per ovary, and oocyte retrieval rate (ORR) among bilateral retrievals with one ovary aspirated by a REI fellow and the contralateral ovary aspirated by a REI faculty physician Outcome measure REI faculty physician REI fellow Difference: fellow–faculty p -value † No. of follicles aspirated < 0.001 Mean (SD) 11.1 (7.2) 13.0 (8.4) 1.9 (5.9) Median (IQR) 10 (6, 15) 11 (7, 17) 2 (− 1, 5) Range 0 to 58 1 to 65 − 25 to 47 No. of oocytes retrieved < 0.001 Mean (SD) 8.1 (5.3) 9.5 (6.2) 1.4 (4.9) Median (IQR) 7 (4, 11) 8 (5, 13) 1 (− 2, 4) Range 0 to 35 0 to 38 − 15 to 23 ORR 0.46 Mean (SD) 0.76 (0.23) 0.76 (0.22) 0.00 (0.24) Median (IQR) 0.80 (0.60, 1.00) 0.79 (0.61, 1.00) 0.00 (− 0.16, 0.13) Range 0 to 1 0 to 1 − 1 to 1 Open in a new tab † Comparisons were evaluated using a two-sided Wilcoxon signed rank test, the non-parametric analog to a paired t -test ‡ There was a single bilateral aspiration in which the REI faculty physician aspirated 0 follicles; however, the REI fellow aspirated 2. The ORR was set to 0 for the REI faculty physician in this instance To address the second study objective, the analysis was limited to seven fellows who performed at least 80 retrievals (range 88–117) in their first year of fellowship. All seven had an average ORR of > 60% in their first 20 retrievals; four of these fellows had an average ORR of > 70%. No fellow dropped below an average ORR of 60% during each of the four chronologically ordered sets of 20 retrievals (Fig. 1 , Supplemental Table 1 ). When the four chronologically ordered sets were compared to the faculty physician mean ORR of 0.76, no significant differences were found ( p -values of 0.69, 0.69, 0.81, and 0.81, respectively). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Fellows’ oocyte retrieval rate (ORR) in the first 80 retrievals during their first year of fellowship. For each fellow ( N = 7), the aspiration/retrieval attempts were chronologically ordered from first to last for their first 80 retrievals. Within each ordered set of 20 aspiration/retrieval attempts, the mean of the 20 ORRs was calculated. A similar calculation was performed for the corresponding faculty Discussion In this study, a comparison of the first bilateral oocyte retrievals from 845 unique patients did not show a significant difference in ORR between fellows versus faculty. All fellows achieved an average ORR of > 60% in their first 20 retrievals. No evidence of a learning curve was seen as all fellows achieved an average ORR > 60% for each of the four chronologically ordered sets of 20 retrievals of their first 80 retrievals. Fellow competency was defined as performance comparable to faculty physician performance; all fellows met this definition for competency after their first 20 retrievals. No significant differences were found when the mean ORR of the fellows in the chronologically ordered sets of 20 retrievals were compared to the faculty physician mean ORR. These data suggest fellows enter fellowship with the skills needed to successfully perform oocyte retrievals following OB/GYN residency. To our knowledge, there have been three studies examining fellow performance of oocyte retrievals that used different methods and reported varied results. In 2011, Goldman et al. conducted a retrospective analysis of REI fellow performance using a proficiency score calculated by dividing the number of oocytes retrieved by the expected number of oocytes. A target proficiency score was calculated based on experienced faculty physicians’ performance. Of the six fellows examined, four reached the target proficiency score by 20 oocyte retrievals, one reached it by 50 retrievals, and one did not achieve the target score. They did not find a statistical difference in the learning curve between the fellows, and there was no statistically significant difference between the average proficiency scores of the fellows compared with the faculty physicians [ 5 ]. These results are similar to the results of our study, which did not demonstrate a learning curve for fellows and showed achievement of clinical proficiency within a relatively low number of retrievals. However, other studies have demonstrated significant variability in learning curves for fellows when using the learning curve–cumulative summation test (LC-CUSUM). Briefly, the LC-CUSUM test detects when a process has reached an adequate predefined level of performance based on prespecified criteria. In a prospective trial completed over 6 months, Dessolle et al. analyzed the learning curves of three OB/GYN residents without prior oocyte retrieval experience using the LC-CUSUM test. Proficiency for trainees was defined as a ratio of oocytes collected to follicles aspirated of ≥ 80% of faculty physicians’ scores. In contrast to our study, they observed significant variability in the number of oocyte retrievals needed by each resident to reach proficiency as defined in the study: one needed 17, one needed 43, and one had not reached proficiency after 50 oocyte retrievals [ 6 ]. In a retrospective study by Verhaeghe et al., the LC-CUSUM test was used to analyze the learning curves of seven residents performing oocyte retrievals. In this study, an ORR was defined as adequate if ≥ 50%, and clinical performance was considered adequate if at least 60% of the retrievals were successful. They found that the number of oocyte retrievals needed to reach clinical proficiency was variable, with four residents reaching this level after 46, 53, 67, and 82 retrievals and three not reaching their definition of clinical proficiency. However, of the five residents who completed a full 6-month rotation, the median number of ovaries aspirated to achieve clinical proficiency was 21, a relatively low number. They concluded that the learning period could be variable due to factors other than the difficulty of the procedure, including the availability of each resident and the duration of their rotation [ 7 ]. As in our study, this study showed that a relatively low number of retrievals might be needed to achieve clinical proficiency. However, in contrast to our study, it also suggests that there can be significant variability in the learning curve to achieve clinical proficiency. In our analysis, we applied the LC-CUSUM test to our data to allow direct comparison to the results of these two previous papers. However, these two papers defined success, failure, and acceptable deviation differently. In addition, we could not identify a formula to replicate the level from either study above which performance is considered adequate [ 6 , 7 ]. Given the substantially disparate results of this test depending on which previous study’s predefined criteria are applied, we have not included the results of this test in our paper. Our study did not demonstrate a difference in the retrieval rates for fellows compared to faculty nor did they show a learning curve for fellows performing oocyte retrievals. In our program, fellows are immediately involved in oocyte retrievals without an observation period. Of the studies previously discussed, two did not detail learning methods for the trainees. In these studies, trainees performed the retrievals under the supervision of faculty physicians. In the study by Verhaeghe et al., trainees first observed faculty physicians performing an unspecified number of retrievals before starting to perform the procedure themselves. In contrast, our data support the involvement of fellows in oocyte retrievals under the supervision of faculty physicians immediately upon the commencement of their fellowship. Early involvement in oocyte retrievals would allow fellows to engage in as many learning opportunities as possible prior to independent practice and achieve proficiency earlier in their training. One training method for fellowship programs that do not immediately involve their fellows in live oocyte retrievals is simulation training. A few studies have examined the role of simulation for trainees in learning oocyte retrieval. In a prospective study, Soave et al. found an increase in speed and accuracy for 32 OB/GYN residents without prior experience in oocyte retrievals after using an oocyte retrieval simulator. They concluded that simulations could be a useful part of initial learning for trainees by allowing them to acquire basic skills prior to performing the procedure on live patients [ 9 ]. In another study, 46 residents participated in an oocyte retrieval simulation workshop, during which 80% successfully completed the clinical scenario. All residents found the training beneficial, and 87% agreed that simulation-based learning should be incorporated into their training program [ 10 ]. While these studies have promising results, neither study examined whether success with simulation led to similar improvements in oocyte retrievals for live patients. Further studies are needed to examine whether simulation training in oocyte retrievals translates to actual clinical practice. Given that our fellows start performing oocyte retrievals in their first year of fellowship, they accrue a large number of oocyte retrievals over the course of their 3-year fellowship. This large number of fellow oocyte retrievals was a major strength of this study. Additionally, by comparing only those retrievals completed by faculty and fellows on the same patient, patient-level confounding factors were controlled for. Furthermore, all oocyte retrievals were completed in the same REI office, therefore limiting inter-clinic variability. Limitations of this study include a lack of data regarding clinical outcomes following oocyte retrievals as well as a lack of categorization of oocytes based on their maturity stage. Following retrievals, oocytes are pooled, so the comparison between faculty and fellow outcomes, such as fertilization rates, was not possible. Our database did not characterize oocytes based on their maturity stage; while this would have given additional information regarding oocyte retrieval outcomes, the comprehensive nature of our follicle aspiration approach provides a robust dataset for evaluating the overall oocyte retrieval rate. Another limitation of this study is the lack of data regarding fellows’ exposure to oocyte retrievals in residency. It is possible that the fellows included in this study had more experience with observation or possibly involvement with oocyte retrievals than is typical for a graduating OB/GYN resident, which may influence these results and limit its generalizability to other fellowship programs. Additionally, the number of follicles aspirated and oocytes retrieved is based on physician counts, which can be subject to error and confirmation bias. Finally, given that fellows choose which side to aspirate first, it is possible that they picked the side that is easier to aspirate. In this study, the fellows aspirated a higher median follicle number and retrieved more oocytes compared to the faculty, which may suggest that fellows typically chose the more robust ovary that was easier to visualize. Given that there was no significant difference in the median ORR between fellows and faculty, it is unlikely that this difference in absolute numbers retrieved is clinically significant. However, although they may have often been aspirating the easier side, these data still support the early involvement of fellows in oocyte retrievals as they did not have a lower ORR compared to faculty. Conclusions Retrieving an adequate number of mature oocytes is a crucial step of IVF; accordingly, it is crucial for REI fellowship programs to train their fellows to perform this skill with expertise. This study showed that the oocyte retrieval rate of fellows did not statistically differ from that of faculty physicians. Additionally, there was no evidence of a learning curve seen for fellows. These data suggest that fellows enter REI fellowship with the skills needed to successfully perform oocyte retrievals following OB/GYN residency and could be involved with oocyte retrievals immediately upon commencement of their fellowship, thereby allowing fellows to engage in as many learning opportunities as possible prior to independent practice. Nonetheless, it is crucial to note that these findings do not eliminate the necessity of fellowship training and supervision, particularly when it comes to both initial and complex cases. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary file1 (DOCX 29 kb) (29.8KB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors gratefully acknowledge the support of the Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility Department at Mayo Clinic. Author contribution Dr. Babayev designed the study, and Dr. Miller completed the data acquisition. Drs. Miller, Babayev, Zhao, and Aase and Ms. Weaver completed data analysis and interpretation. Ms. Weaver and Dr. Li completed statistical analyses and designed the tables and figures. Dr. Aase drafted the manuscript, and Drs. Miller, Babayev, Zhao, Aase, and Li and Ms. Weaver edited the manuscript. Drs. Miller, Babayev, Zhao, Aase, and Li and Ms. Weaver all approved the final version to be submitted. Data availability The data underlying this article will be shared on reasonable request to the corresponding author. Declarations Ethics approval The Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board reviewed this retrospective cohort study and determined it to be exempt from the requirement for IRB approval (IRB#: 21–003449). Consent to participate All included participants gave consent for their medical records to be utilized for research purposes. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. CDC. ART success rates. Available at: https://www.cdc.gov/art/artdata/index.html . Accessed May 27, 2022. 2. Polyzos NP, Drakopoulos P, Parra J, Pellicer A, Santos-Ribeiro S, Tournaye H, et al. Cumulative live birth rates according to the number of oocytes retrieved after the first ovarian stimulation for in vitro fertilization/intracytoplasmic sperm injection: a multicenter multinational analysis including ∼15,000 women. Fertil Steril. 2018;110:661-70.e1. 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2018.04.039 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Minimum standards for practices offering assisted reproductive technologies: a committee opinion. Fertil Steril 2020;113:536-41 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 4. D''Angelo A, Panayotidis C, Amso N, Marci R, Matorras R, Onofriescu M et al. Recommendations for good practice in ultrasound: oocyte pick up(†). Hum Reprod Open 2019;2019:hoz025. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 5. Goldman KN, Moon KS, Yauger BJ, Payson MD, Segars JH, Stegmann BJ. Proficiency in oocyte retrieval: how many procedures are necessary for training? Fertil Steril. 2011;95:2279–82. 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2011.02.055 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Dessolle L, Leperlier F, Biau DJ, Fréour T, Barrière P. Proficiency in oocyte retrieval assessed by the learning curve cumulative summation test. Reprod Biomed Online. 2014;29:187–92. 10.1016/j.rbmo.2014.03.016 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Verhaeghe C, El Hachem H, Inchboard L, Corroenne R, Dreux C, Jeanneteau P, et al. Assessment of operator performance during oocyte retrievals: residents’ learning curve and continuous monitoring of senior physicians. BMC Med Educ. 2021;21:193. 10.1186/s12909-021-02615-w [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education. Frequently asked questions: institutional requirements ACGME. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/pdfs/faq/ir_faqs.pdf . Accessed October 30, 2022. 9. Soave I, D’Angelo A, Piva I, Marci R. A pilot study on oocyte retrieval simulator: a new tool for training? J Med Syst. 2019;43:202. 10.1007/s10916-019-1340-3 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Corroenne R, Legendre G, Riethmuller D, El-Hachem H, Zhu KH, Dreux C, et al. Benefits of an oocyte pickup simulation training program using a high-fidelity simulator (PickUpSim(TM)) for obstetrics and gynaecology residents: an observational survey study. J Obstet Gynaecol. 2021;41:631–6. 10.1080/01443615.2020.1789951 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary file1 (DOCX 29 kb) (29.8KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data underlying this article will be shared on reasonable request to the corresponding author.',135,'2025-10-09 07:37:15','2025-10-09 07:37:15');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(899,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6555143','Embryo transfer simulation improves pregnancy rates and decreases time to proficiency in Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility fellow embryo transfers.','Heitmann RJ, Hill MJ, Csokmay JM, Pilgrim J, DeCherney AH, Deering S.','2017','airline iui presimulation molds recreate perineum perineum speculum fasteners heitmann straps pliable perineum speculum speculum heitmann smiths embryologists intracytoplasmic intracytoplasmic thaw afterload iui sonogram afterload posttrainer embryologists embryologist heitmann solves baselines posttrainer pretrainer heitmann posttrainer pretrainer posttrainer pretrainer brezina iuis iui iuis iui iui iui iui ultrasonographer embryologist nihms1026166 keegan cae segars eunice shriver franc deering hodor wylen poggi satin deering auguste lockrow rackow solnik senapati pozolo moldovanu tarcoveanu dimofte lupascu bradea jsls wittenberger catherino papageorgiou hearns leondires cruess hearns creuss segars karande rinehart gleicher cooks spoil uyar bener ciray bahceci desparoir capelle banet noizet gamerre courbiere heitmann schimmel segars csokmay csokmay chason hennessy chason payson segars csokmay neithardt segars hennessy mckeeby afterloading acgmeweb common_program_requirements_07012011 gonzalo heist ferenchick appleby tikkinen tuomivaara tapanainen martikainen vansteelandt elst coetsier dhont sutter alvero hearns catherino leondires segars brezina wallach kolp zacur s284 missmer racowsky inseminations nihms1026166','Abstract Objective To design and evaluate an ET simulator to train Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility (REI) fellows’ techniques of ET. Design Simulation model development and retrospective cohort analysis. Setting Not applicable. Patient(s) Patients undergoing IVF. Intervention(s) Simulation model evaluation and implementation of ET simulation training. Main Outcome Measure(s) Pregnancy rates. Result(s) The REI fellow and faculty evaluation responses (n = 19/21 [90%]) of the model demonstrated realistic characteristics, with evaluators concluding the model was suitable for training in almost all evaluated areas. A total of 12 REI fellows who performed ET were analyzed: 6 before ET trainer and 6 after ET trainer. Pregnancy rates were 31% in the initial 10 ETs per fellow before simulator vs. 46% after simulator. One of six pre–ET trainer fellows (17%) had pregnancy rates ≥40% in their first 10 ETs; whereas four of six post–ET trainer fellows had pregnancy rates ≥40% in their first 10 ETs. The average number of ETs to obtain >40% pregnancy efficiency was 27 ETs before trainer vs. 15 ETs after trainer. Pregnancy rates were similar in the two groups after 20 ETs, and collective terminal pregnancy rates were >50% after 40 ETs. Conclusion(s) Embryo transfer simulation improved REI fellow pregnancy rates in their first 10 transfers and led to a more rapid ET proficiency. These data suggest potential value in adopting ET simulation, even in programs with a robust history of live ET in fellowship training. Keywords: Embryo transfer, fellowship education, medical simulation training, model development, pregnancy rates Simulation training has been used to train professionals in difficult aspects of their jobs for many decades. The airline industry and military have long used flight simulators in training their pilots. Simulation offers the opportunity to train in numerous situations and is often used to address high-risk or high-stakes procedures. Similarly, nearly all medical specialties use some aspect of simulation to teach medical students, residents, and fellows important procedures, with proven effectiveness ( 1 – 4 ). Simulation training is increasingly being used in the training of medical professionals in their specialty or subspecialty field and has been developed for even the most experienced surgeon to “warm up” before surgical procedures ( 5 ). During Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility (REI) fellowship, development of two important technical skills is essential for independent practice: transvaginal oocyte retrieval and ET. Previous studies have demonstrated the importance of training in the success of the ET ( 6 , 7 ). In addition, significant variance has been shown to occur between individual providers ( 8 – 11 ), confirming the importance of standardized training for physicians. Despite the importance of effective training in these procedures, not all fellowship programs provide experience in live ET, instead using IUI and mock transfers as surrogates for teaching these techniques. Viewing ET as the more difficult and important of the two skills, we focused on constructing a simulation model for this specific procedure. Our objectives were, first, to design and construct a prototype simulation model that would adequately and realistically replicate the skills needed to perform an ET procedure. Second, we evaluated pregnancy outcomes after IVF in fellows conducting ET both before and after simulation trainer implementation. We hypothesized use of ET simulation would lead to improved fellow pregnancy rates and shorten the time to procedure proficiency compared with presimulation training. MATERIALS AND METHODS Model Development Model development occurred from July 2013 through April 2014. A simulation uterine model was obtained from CEA Healthcare. This model was created using molds that allow for the provider to perform an ultrasound, evaluate the uterine cavity, and visualize the actual insertion of the catheter during the procedure. It includes both the soft tissue of the abdomen and a separate uterus ( Fig. 1A ). Care was taken to recreate the dimensions and characteristics expected in a woman who would be undergoing the procedure. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Model development demonstrating ( A ) uterine model in gel mold, ( B ) female perineum, and ( C ) female simulation pelvis. ( D ) The perineum was attached to the pelvic model to allow for speculum placement. ( E ) Hook and loop fasteners were placed to secure the uterine mold in the pelvis. Heitmann. Embryo transfer simulation. Fertil Steril 2017. Model construction is demonstrated in Figures 1A – E and 2A – D . The uterine model was placed into an empty pelvic simulation model and attached to the vaginal canal using hook and loop straps. A pliable female perineum was attached to the pelvic model to simulate the normal female external anatomy. This additionally allowed for placement of a speculum for visualization of the cervical os of the uterine model. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab ( A ) Completed uterine model within the pelvic simulator. ( B ) Cervical view after speculum placement provided a realistic vaginal/cervical view. ( C ) Transabdominal ultrasound assessment of the uterus. ( D ) Representative view of uterus on transvaginal ultrasound. An ET catheter is visualized in the uterine cavity ( yellow arrow ). Heitmann. Embryo transfer simulation. Fertil Steril 2017. To achieve the uterine lining sonographic characteristics, the uterine cavity was filled with 10 mL of ultrasound gel. Transabdominal ultrasound was performed using a 2.5–5.0–mHz probe. Catheter visualization was best achieved with use of a 23-cm Wallace Sure View (Smiths Medical) trial catheter. After construction of the model, evaluation of the simulator was performed by IVF nurses, embryologists, REI fellows, and attending physicians, all of whom were familiar with IVF-ET procedures. Each participant was oriented to the model and then performed practice procedures on the simulator as many times as desired. A two-page evaluation scoring sheet using a 5-point Likert scale (1 = not realistic, 5 = very realistic) and additional questions were used to assess performance and obtain feedback. Evaluations were completed anonymously. The model development study was approved by the institutional review board at the National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD. Retrospective Cohort Study A retrospective cohort analysis of all fresh, autologous, IVF and intracytoplasmic sperm injection cycles performed at Walter Reed National Military Medical Center (Bethesda, MD) was done. A 2-year time period (July 2012–June 2014) of REI fellow ETs before implementation of ET simulation was compared with a 2-year period (July 2014–June 2016) after implementation of ET simulation. This retrospective review and data analysis was collected as part of routine clinical care and approved as an exempt protocol by the Walter Reed National Military Medical Center institutional review board. Patients included were military healthcare beneficiaries, aged 18–42 years, undergoing fresh, autologous IVF or intracytoplasmic sperm injection cycles during the study time period. Those patients undergoing frozen–thaw ET cycles were excluded because fellows do not normally perform these ETs. No donor oocyte or donor embryo cycles are performed at our facility. Controlled ovarian stimulation, final oocyte maturation trigger, ET timing and number, P supplementation, and hCG testing were done using protocols previously described ( 12 – 15 ). Embryo transfer was performed under ultrasound guidance using the afterload technique ( 16 ). During the entire study period, fellows were required to perform 30 mock ETs, such as during IUI or saline sonogram using ET catheters, before transfer of live embryos. Fellows were also required to perform transabdominal ultrasounds during ET and demonstrate complete knowledge of the afterload ET technique before their first ET. The posttrainer group received additional training on the ET trainer. This training included gentle placement of a Wallace catheter through the cervix, visualization of the catheter by a faculty physician, advancement of the catheter to 1–2 cm from the fundus, and good communication of catheter depth and location with the faculty. The primary outcome evaluated was pregnancy, defined as an elevated quantitative hCG level 2 weeks after oocyte retrieval, because events after implantation were not considered to be affected by the ET technique. To control for the potential effect of traumatic ETs on pregnancy loss, additional outcomes included chemical pregnancy, miscarriage, and ongoing pregnancy. Ongoing pregnancy was defined as fetal cardiac activity on transvaginal ultrasound at 7 weeks’ gestation. Individual fellow pregnancy rates and collective pregnancy rates were calculated per increments of 10 ETs. Descriptive and analytical statistical analysis was performed using Microsoft Excel (Microsoft Office 2010) and SPSS version 22 (IBM), respectively. Chi-squared analysis was performed to compare pregnancy rates between the two groups. Multivariate logistic regression accounted for patient age, embryo quality, embryo stage, and the number of embryos transferred. A P value of <.05 was considered statistically significant. RESULTS Model Development A total of 19 of 21 possible participants’ evaluations (90%) were anonymously completed. The majority of evaluations (14 [74%]) were completed by either fellow or attending physicians, whereas 3 respondents (16%) were experienced IVF nurses and 2 (10%) were embryologists. Although the IVF nurses and embryologist do not perform ET they do participate as part of the transfer team. As such they evaluated all aspects of the model except for the two questions evaluating passage of the ET catheter. Seven (50%) of physicians had performed more than 100 ETs during their career; three (21%) had performed 0–10 transfers; one (7%) had performed 11–30 transfers; two (14%) had performed 31–50 transfers; and one (7%) had performed 51–100 transfers. Overall the model scored very well in all aspects of evaluation. The overall replication of procedure scored an average (±SEM) 4.8 ± 0.1 (range, 4–5). Uterine appearance (4.5 ± 0.2; range, 2–5), catheter visualization (4.2 ± 0.2; range, 2–5), feel of catheter passage (4.6 ± 0.1; range, 3–5), ease of catheter passage (4.4 ± 0.2; range, 3–5), and ability for team participation (4.9 ± 0.05; range, 4–5) all scored very favorably. The lowest-scoring aspect of evaluation was lining visualization (3.8 ± 0.3; range, 1–5) ( Fig. 3 ). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Histograms demonstrating scoring in various different model evaluation categories. 1 = not realistic, 5 = very realistic. Heitmann. Embryo transfer simulation. Fertil Steril 2017. When evaluating the written feedback from the additional questions, positive and negative aspects of the model were assessed. Positive comments included, “perfect for learning catheter set up and intrauterine position”; “portable”; “easy to set up”; “life-like”; and “solves inherent problem with training.” Negative comments or areas of improvement included, “vagina too long”; “uterus too long”; “catheter can tend to ride along the top of the intrauterine gel”; “passage too easy”; and “only life-like for a small percentage of patients.” Retrospective Cohort Study The two cohorts were similar in patient baselines and embryo characteristics between the pre- and posttrainer cohorts ( Supplemental Table 1 , available online). The overall pregnancy rate over the study period was 55.4%. Fifty-five percent (55%) of ETs were performed by fellows and 45% by faculty. Pregnancy rates were 31% in the initial 10 ETs per fellow before simulator vs. 46% after simulator ( P =.03). One of six pretrainer fellows had a pregnancy rate >40% in their first 10 ETs; whereas four of six post-ET trainer fellows had pregnancy rates ≥40% in their first 10 ETs. The average number of ETs to obtain ≥40% pregnancy efficiency was 27 ETs before trainer vs. 15 ETs after trainer ( P =.03). Pregnancy rates were similar in the two groups after 20 ETs, and collective terminal pregnancy rates were >50% after 40 ETs in both groups ( Fig. 4 ). FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Pregnancy rates (%) were increased and time to proficiency was decreased after implementation of ET simulation. * P <.05. Heitmann. Embryo transfer simulation. Fertil Steril 2017. Multivariate logistic regression controlled for patient age, embryo quality, embryo stage, and the number of embryos transferred. The multivariate model demonstrated that posttrainer fellows had a higher pregnancy rate than pretrainer fellows during the first 10 ETs (odds ratio 3.10, 95% confidence interval 1.11–8.78), but both groups were similar after 20 ETs were performed. In analysis of clinical pregnancy, the multivariate model demonstrated that posttrainer fellows had a higher clinical pregnancy rate than pretrainer fellows during the first 10 ETs (odds ratio 4.2, 95% confidence interval 1.24–14.15), but both groups were similar after 20 ETs were performed. The incidence of biochemical pregnancy and miscarriage was analyzed in the first 10 transfers for each fellow. Of the initial pregnancies with a positive hCG, 78.7% progressed to ongoing pregnancies. Of the pregnancies lost before ultrasound, one was an ectopic pregnancy, and the remainder were chemical pregnancies. The percentage of pregnancies ending in a biochemical loss was similar before and after the ET trainer (23.8% before vs. 14.8% after, P =.47). Of pregnancies resulting in an ongoing pregnancy, 12.5% resulted in miscarriage. The percentage of pregnancies ending in miscarriage was also similar before and after the ET trainer (9.5% before vs. 14.8% after, P =.68). The incidence of biochemical pregnancy and miscarriage remained similar between the two groups in their remaining transfers after the first 10. DISCUSSION Although no argument can be made against the acquisition of medical skills and learning on “real live” patients, there are an increasing number of barriers impeding the adequate use of such training modalities. Increased restrictions on resident work hours ( 17 ), decreased bedside teaching ( 18 ), and need for attending participation/completion of medical procedures for reimbursement purposes have all led to an overall detrimental impact on medical education. Consequently the use of simulation training has sharply risen over the past few years. A report by the Association of American Medical Colleges commented on the increasing use of simulation in not only obstetrics and gynecology but in other areas of evaluation in medical schools ( 19 ). This report also commented on the potential ability to improve patient safety and overall healthcare in general. The American Congress of Obstetricians and Gynecologists has recognized the emerging importance of simulation training of residents and fellows by developing the American Congress of Obstetricians and Gynecologists Simulations Working Group, which in 2012 finalized a comprehensive set of learning objectives and simulation instructions for program directors. Embryo transfer training has long been recognized to be an important aspect of fellowship training in REI. Many factors affect the results after ET, including patient age, infertility duration ( 20 ), number and quality of embryos transferred ( 21 ), level of difficulty ( 22 , 23 ), catheter type ( 24 ), and presence of blood on the transfer catheter ( 25 ). Physician skill and experience with ET has been shown to be another important factor in pregnancy rates after IVF ( 8 – 11 ). It has been shown that fellow performance of a critical number of ETs is needed to attain levels of proficiency (pregnancy rates) commensurate with attending physicians ( 7 ). These factors undoubtedly contribute to the decision for fellowship programs to allow or not allow their fellows to perform live ET during their training years. Given the high-stakes outcome of the procedure, only a handful of programs allow their fellows to participate in “live” ETs. A single-institution survey done in 2012 demonstrated the lack of ET training among REI fellowship graduates. Brezina et al. ( 26 ) found that 59% of trainees received zero “live” ET experiences during their fellowship. This group of respondents was more likely to require moderate to heavy training by their colleagues after training and were more likely to feel that ET training should be incorporated in training, compared with their counterparts who received ET training ( 26 ). Currently limited alternative options exist to train fellows with the skills needed to perform ET other than performance of live ET. Some have substituted or augmented ET training by performing IUIs with the use of an ET catheter. Although this type of training can simulate the required technical steps it does not fully replicate the entire procedure. Shah et al. ( 27 ) found that mock ET via IUI did not increase pregnancy rates or decrease time to proficiency compared with non-mock transfer IUIs. Additionally, the performance of an abdominal ultrasound-guided IUI is not usually done to give the fellow the visual feedback and practice of seeing the catheter advance to the correct location required for ET. The results by Shah et al. ( 27 ) would suggest that the comparison group of fellows performing mock ET with IUI is not adequate because IUI mock transfers demonstrated no benefit. This has the potential to bias the results toward simulation training. However, comparison with a group with no prior ET training may present an even larger bias. The simulation model developed and analyzed in this study is different from mock ETs performed via IUI. This model more accurately and completely replicates the steps, process, and visualization experienced with live ET. Almost certainly the best training comes with performance of live ET, but the majority of fellows in training do not receive this experience ( 26 ). This simulation model can act as a surrogate to enhance fellowship learning and training. We recognize current limitations with this model development because a simulator cannot completely replicate every patient’s anatomy or replace the important nature of performing live ET. However, with development of an ET simulator, fellows can be given training in the process and techniques of ET before performance in a live situation. Participation by other members of the assisted reproductive technology team (e.g., ultrasonographer, embryologist) can also be incorporated into the training model. Additionally, although the model was a prototype and had some improvements to be considered high fidelity, it was constructed with simplicity, portability, and expense in mind. These factors can offer an alternative for programs in which a more-advanced or higher-cost model is not feasible. The number of fellows evaluated by the study is small, and the number of evaluated fellows decreased as the number of ETs increased. However, despite this we believe that the benefit of simulation is demonstrated. As more training programs adopt this training paradigm larger, multicenter, randomized, controlled trials will be able to be completed. In conclusion, herein we report that the development and, most importantly, implementation of a medical simulation model for teaching ET techniques led to improved pregnancy rates and decreased time to proficiency for trainees, even in a high-volume ET fellowship. This model provided a realistic tactile experience, which providers felt was realistic enough to allow for enhanced teaching for fellows before participation in “real-life” ET. This simulation model shifts the proficiency curve to the left, allowing fellows to obtain ET proficiency earlier. This is clinically important, especially if you are a patient receiving an ET from a provider early on in their experience. Given the potential benefits of this type of training, we believe that REI fellowship training should include the implementation of ET trainers. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1026166-supplement-1.pdf (21.8KB, pdf) Acknowledgments The authors thank Brian Keegan at CAE Healthcare for assisting in the prototype development. There were no financial relationships or funds transferred in the development of this simulation model. CEA Healthcare had no participation in the evaluation of the model or the manuscript writing and approval. The authors also thank Dr. James Segars, the staff of the A.R.T. Institute of Washington, Inc., and all the REI fellows at the National Institutes of Health/Walter Reed program for their support of ET simulation training. Supported, in part, by the intramural research program of the Program in Reproductive and Adult Endocrinology, Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development, National Institutes of Health. Footnotes R.J.H. has nothing to disclose. M.J.H. has nothing to disclose. J.M.C. has nothing to disclose. J.P. has nothing to disclose. A.H.D. has nothing to disclose. S.D. has nothing to disclose. REFERENCES 1. Franc JM, Nichols D, Dong SL. Increasing emergency medicine residents’ confidence in disaster management: use of an emergency department simulator and an expedited curriculum. Prehosp Disaster Med 2012;27:31–5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Deering SH, Hodor JG, Wylen M, Poggi S, Nielsen PE, Satin AJ. Additional training with an obstetric simulator improves medical student comfort with basic procedures. Simul Healthc 2006;1:32–4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Deering S, Auguste T, Lockrow E. Obstetric simulation for medical student, resident, and fellow education. Semin Perinatol 2013;37:143–5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Rackow BW, Solnik MJ, Tu FF, Senapati S, Pozolo KE, Du H. Deliberate practice improves obstetrics and gynecology residents’ hysteroscopy skills. J Grad Med Educ 2012;4:329–34. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Moldovanu R, Tarcoveanu E, Dimofte G, Lupascu C, Bradea C. Preoperative warm-up using a virtual reality simulator. JSLS 2011;15:533–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Wittenberger MD, Catherino WH, Armstrong AY. Role of embryo transfer in fellowship training. Fertil Steril 2007;88:1014–5. [ DOI ] [ PMC free artic